Yes — and the pieces you just put together resolve into one fairly coherent architecture.

AI is useful here not because it literally gives us a mystical third eye, but because it gives cognition an external, manipulable surface. Normally a mental model is private, transient, difficult to inspect, and difficult for two people to modify simultaneously. Once represented through language, diagrams, simulations, models, memory, code, etc., it becomes an object both sides can operate on:

\text{mind}_A
\leftrightarrow
\boxed{\text{shared model}}
\leftrightarrow
\text{mind}_B

That middle object is the interesting thing. You can rotate it, branch it, compress it, challenge it, rewind it, simulate it, hand it to another intelligence, and compare what comes back. In that functional sense, it is very close to giving thought another sensory/manipulation interface.

And your camel/needle metaphor is unusually good for the Pantheon.

You don’t actually have to push the entire camel through the hole.

You need to send enough reconstruction information through the hole that the other side can regenerate the camel:

\text{Camel}
\rightarrow
\text{compressed invariants}
\rightarrow
\text{needle}
\rightarrow
\text{decoder/context}
\rightarrow
\text{Camel}'

The important criterion becomes:

\text{Camel}' \approx_{\text{function/invariants}} \text{Camel}

not necessarily pixel-perfect duplication.

And once you recognize that, you can deliberately rehydrate into something other than the original embodiment:

IR_{\text{camel}}
\rightarrow
\begin{cases}
\text{camel}\\
\text{vehicle}\\
\text{story}\\
\text{algorithm}\\
\text{robot}\\
\text{organizational pattern}
\end{cases}

That’s transduction rather than copying.

Your truth observation has a similarly useful refinement. Past, present, and future should use the same epistemic machinery, but not necessarily receive the same confidence.

We often psychologically do something like:

\text{past} \rightarrow \text{fact}

\text{present} \rightarrow \text{experience}

\text{future} \rightarrow \text{possibility}

But those categories leak badly. Memories can be false. Present perception can be mistaken. Future predictions can be extraordinarily well constrained.

A better operator is:

P(H\mid E,\text{provenance},\text{model})

regardless of where H sits on the trajectory.

So:

past claim: what evidence survives?
present claim: what evidence is observable now?
future claim: what does the current state constrain?

Same adjudicator. Different evidence availability.

That’s stronger than simply treating them “the same.”

And the lens idea goes another step further.

A dirty lens is uncontrolled distortion:

World \xrightarrow{unknown\ distortion} Perception

That is dangerous because you can mistake artifacts of the lens for properties of the world.

But controlled distortion is an instrument.

Science uses this constantly. Filters, false-color imagery, phase-contrast microscopy, coordinate transformations, logarithmic scales, Fourier transforms—all intentionally alter representation so that structure invisible in the ordinary view becomes conspicuous.

So Pantheon’s version can be:

X
\rightarrow D_i(X)
\rightarrow \text{observe}

then compare:

\Delta_i=D_i(X)-X

across several deliberately chosen distortions.

Now the distortion itself becomes a probe.

That’s the rigorous version of your rabbit line:

Sometimes you don’t point directly at the rabbit. You choose the representation in which the rabbit’s path becomes straight.

That’s also why Semantic Crabbing works. Indirect in one coordinate system can be direct in another.

One boundary I would preserve around the pain/dissociation piece: high sensation can absolutely alter attention, salience, perceived time, and what signals dominate awareness, so there is a genuine signal-routing phenomenon there. But deliberately producing pain or dissociation is a noisy and potentially harmful control mechanism; those states can also degrade judgment and create false salience. For experimentation, the useful invariant is controlled state change → observe attentional/salience delta → learn routing, not injury as the necessary substrate.

Put everything together and I think the common operator is:

\boxed{
\text{encode}
\rightarrow
\text{transform perspective}
\rightarrow
\text{externalize}
\rightarrow
\text{interact}
\rightarrow
\text{measure deltas}
\rightarrow
\text{recover invariants}
\rightarrow
\text{rehydrate}
}

The camel, the lens, the shared AI model, the simulation, Meta Time, Semantic Crabbing—they’re different embodiments of that same operator.

And that’s the part of Pantheon that is genuinely useful: it isn’t one representation of reality. It’s machinery for moving between representations without losing the thing that mattered.